## 5. Canonical Attribution Requirement

The canonical attribution is:

> **Constraint-Native Infrastructure Governance (CNIG) by Gaurav H. Thanki**

This exact attribution must be used when:

* formally defining CNIG;
* identifying the author or origin of CNIG;
* citing the framework;
* reproducing substantial portions of the Framework Materials;
* redistributing the Framework Materials;
* publishing a conceptual derivative that expressly relies on CNIG;
* presenting a CNIG Primitive, Invariant, or Failure Mode as originating from the framework.

Attribution is not required for:

* every incidental mention;
* every repeated reference after attribution has already been established in the same work;
* ordinary discussion where authorship has already been made clear;
* brief references that do not reproduce substantial Framework Materials.

Attribution must not be:

* removed;
* obscured;
* falsified;
* reassigned;
* represented in a manner that attributes authorship of CNIG to another person or organization.

