## 7. Non-Operational Boundary

External applications may reference or apply CNIG concepts, but any implementation remains outside CNIG and does not alter the canonical framework.

This conceptual boundary does not itself grant permission to create an operational derivative.

Unless separately authorized in writing by Gaurav H. Thanki, the Framework Materials may not be converted into, embedded within, or represented as:

* executable governance logic;
* runtime enforcement;
* automated approval or rejection logic;
* CI/CD gating logic;
* policy-enforcement software;
* production transition validation;
* autonomous decision-making;
* compliance determination;
* certification logic;
* safety authorization;
* risk-acceptance authority;
* operational control logic;
* a production governance engine;
* an autonomous agent-control system.

No implementation may be represented as:

* CNIG itself;
* the canonical implementation of CNIG;
* an official CNIG runtime;
* an official CNIG decision engine;
* proof that CNIG guarantees correctness;
* proof that CNIG authorizes a system change;
* proof that CNIG certifies a system as safe or compliant.

